{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of upward momentum: The model consistently fails to capture strong bull runs (e.g., July 2022, Jan 2023), predicting flat or slightly rising trends while true prices surge 20-30%. Apply a positive bias adjustment when recent history shows a sustained upward trajectory (>3 consecutive days of gains) regardless of low sentiment scores.",
    "Address excessive mean reversion bias: The model assumes prices will revert to a narrow band (e.g., ~76-84) immediately after volatility, ignoring structural regime shifts. When history ends with a sharp drop followed by a recovery, the model underestimates the new higher equilibrium level.",
    "Incorporate sentiment lag: Positive sentiment spikes (e.g., 0.40-0.50) often precede price rallies that the model misses entirely. If sentiment history shows values >0.20 within the last 5 days, increase the forecast slope by at least 15-20% to account for delayed market reaction.",
    "Reduce flat-line forecasts during high-volatility regimes: The model's 30-day horizon is too smooth. In periods of high variance (standard deviation > 5), flatten the forecast curve and introduce a wider confidence interval or a step-function increase to match the true outcome's volatility.",
    "Adjust for overshoot correction: After a major spike (e.g., hitting 90+), the model predicts a slow, linear decline. In reality, prices often stabilize at a higher plateau before correcting. Avoid predicting a return to pre-spike lows; instead, anchor the forecast to the new higher mean."
  ]
}
